{
 "_README": [
  "WHERE EACH ROOM ACTUALLY IS, in fractions of the whole sheet, read off the plan by eye against",
  "a 5 % grid (exp-truth-boxes.py grid <stem>). This is the only ground truth in the scan tooling:",
  "everything else scores the review boxes against each other, which cannot answer 'does the box",
  "sit on the room'. Worth about a percent either way — enough to separate 'on the room' from",
  "'half on the neighbour', not enough to rank two drawings a point apart.",
  "Labels are the reader's own captions, so a drawing can be matched to a room. Where a sheet",
  "hlv9vurn and plan-007 were marked 10 Sep 2026 for the wall snap: the two furnished renders where the snap most often landed on furniture. Open-plan sides with no wall (drawing/dining, living hall/drawing) are marked where the reader put them.",
  "repeats a caption (three balconies), the match is made on the READER's rectangle, which is the",
  "same for every drawing being compared, so no drawing can win by claiming a different room.",
  "ADD A SHEET ONLY BY LOOKING AT IT. Never copy these from a model reply — that is the thing",
  "being judged.",
  "floor-plan-1 (the owner's own flat) was marked 19 Sep 2026 off the 5 % grid, for the fused-balcony defect. The three right/top BALCONY captions are marked as the three separate strips they are; the open living/dining is marked to the walls that do bound it and to the reader's edge where none does.",
  "greencourt-526, plan-006, plan-010, towerEF-1854 and plan-034 were marked 29 Sep 2026 off the 5 % grid (direction trial: more sheets behind 'score on the page, not the grid'). Each room to its own walls; an open side (a kitchen or lobby open to its dining space) where the printed size puts it. Every sheet was drawn back with `show` and looked at before it was kept. Captions only were taken from the reader, so a drawing can be matched to a room; no position was."
 ],
